2 CONC on Smør, grandson of Jon Halvardson, but continued from the female side in the Rømer family, among others.     The coat of arms was a gold leopard head under a red spear on a blue field. On the helmet are three ostrich plumes and 
2 CONC three gold morning stars.     The founder of the family, Sir Jon Ragnvaldson Smør, created a shield with a diagonal bar for himself in 1297.  The shield is from Sir Jon's seal in 1458. Jon Ragnvaldson Smør, believed born around 12
2 CONC 40, died in 1328, inasmuch as a 'soul mass' was held in Old Church in Nordfjord.  He was on the Royal council (of Eirik II 'Priesthalter" and Håkon V), a knight, and one of the important men in the kingdom.  P. A. Munch states tha
2 CONC t Jon was likely the son of Ragnvald Urka, who attended King Håkon Håkonson 'the old' IV on his voyage to Scotland in 1263 and that Ragnvald was also the father of Aslak Ragnvaldson who lived at the same time as Jon.     Jon was g
2 CONC overnor in Bergen, Norway's capital at that time.  He was the king's highest ranking official, maintained order, sat in the courts and collected the king's income.  In 1295 he was at a meeting in the palace in Denmark during peace
2 CONC  negotiations between Norway and Denmark.  He helped create a pact between King Eirik II and Archbishop Jørund of Nideros.  In 1305 he witnessed the dowry of Queen Euphemia.  In 1297 he was in Trøndelag involved in several importa
2 CONC nt cases on his own behalf or as the king's regent.  He is mentioned with other noblemen.     He owned much property in outer Sundefjord which he apparently leased to Munkeliv Cloister for five years in 1300.  He never reclaimed h
2 CONC is property. Some believe it was an inheritance from his father and that he actually sold it because he lived mostly in Bergen and as an ambassador he traveled so much, that he was unable to look after his property as he became ol
2 CONC der.
