1 NAME William 4 /of Torgar/
2 GIVN William 4
2 SURN of Torgar
2 NICK Herr Viljalm av Torgar
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1260
2 ADDR
3 STAE Nordland
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1308
2 ADDR
3 STAE Nordland
3 CTRY Norway
1 OCCU Knight, Diplomat
1 TITL Lord
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''BIOGRAPHY'''
2 CONT
2 CONT William IV of Torgar was likely born around 1260. Based on name, place, and generation time, William IV may have been a paternal grandson of Baron William III of Torgar (born c. 1190, dead c. 1240). In 1277, the title of Knight ('
2 CONC 'ridder'') was introduced in Norway. William IV was knighted no later than 1290, when he is mentioned with the title of Lord (''Herr'').
2 CONT
2 CONT Also no later than 1290, William IV married Brynhild Olavsdotter of Steine. His (or their) son was Eirik Viljalmson of Torgar (died c. 1333), married to Cecilia Håkonsdotter Bolt (died c. in the 1390s) in a childless marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems like Eirik was named in honour of King Eirik II, Lady Brynhild's second cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding a man at Torgar known as Kolbein Viljalmson, he was more likely a paternal grandson rather than a son of Lord William and Lady Brynhild, meaning that William and Brynhild must have had a son Viljalm Viljalmson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike his ancestors, William IV did not become a baron. He has incorrectly been identified as the baron called 'Velent af Stikla' (fl. 1305), but that's not our William IV but the Englishman Weland de Stiklaw. Nonetheless, Willia
2 CONC m IV had an impressive career, first serving as royal envoy to England and Scotland during the Scottish succession crisis.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''HISTORICAL RECORDS'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter dated to 1290, Magnhild Hallkjellsdotter (Krøkedans) rents a part of the farm Vik to Erik, Abbot of Munkeliv for five years. Her witnesses are (translated into English) 'Lord Aslak, my in-law, and Lord William and Lady
2 CONC  Brynhild, my daughter' (''herra Aslakir maghir min oc herra Villialmir oc frw Brynildir dottir min'').
2 CONT
2 CONT In a similar letter dated to 6 February 1288, Magnhild Hallkjellsdotter applies 'the seal of my Olaf' (''incigle Olafs mins''), i.e., her husband Olav Ragndidarson of Stein, suggesting that Brynhild's full name may have been Brynh
2 CONC ild Olavsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In ''Aslak Bolts jordebok'', compiled in the 1430s, it says that Lord William and Lady Brynhild donated land to the Archbishop of Nidaros, confirming that William and Brynhild were married. The name Brynhild appears among Lord Wil
2 CONC liam's descendants at Torgar, namely, Brynhild Kolbeinsdotter (fl. c. 1402-1428).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:''' Diplomatarium Norvegicum, vol. XII, no. 13. https://www.dokpro.uio.no/cgi-bin/middelalder/diplom_vise_tekst.cgi?b=10498&s=n&str=
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter dated to 2 July 1293 in Berwick-upon-Tweed, Knight William of Torgar (''Willielmus de Thornham Militem'') is mentioned as the fourth of King Eric II of Norway's five representatives sent to King Edward I of England to 
2 CONC negotiate in an inheritance dispute over the vacant Scottish throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:''' Rotuli Parliamenti 1293. 20 Edw. I
2 CONT '''Source:''' Diplomatarium Norvegicum, vol. IXX, no. 372. https://www.dokpro.uio.no/cgi-bin/middelalder/diplom_vise_tekst.cgi?b=16347&s=n&str=
2 CONT '''Source:''' Diplomatarium Norvegicum, vol. IXX, no. 378. https://www.dokpro.uio.no/cgi-bin/middelalder/diplom_vise_tekst.cgi?b=16353&s=n&str=
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter dated to 18 May 1301 in Nidaros (Trondheim), Arne, Abbot of Nidarholm, records a testimony regarding the dispute between the Chapter of St Olaf's See (Archdiocese of Nidaros) and Lord William of Torgar (''herrs Vilialm
2 CONC s j Torgom'') over Haug Church in Verdal.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:''' Diplomatarium Norvegicum, vol. III, nr. 47. https://www.dokpro.uio.no/cgi-bin/middelalder/diplom_vise_tekst.cgi?b=2334&s=n&str=
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter dated to 28 Oct 1308 in Westminster, King Edward II of England writes to King Haakon V of Norway, telling that one Peter de Thornden, a merchant of King's Lynn, claims that his ships and goods have been robbed by Willi
2 CONC am of Torgar from Trondheim (''Willelmus de Torwh de Thorndeyn'') and four other aristocrats.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:''' Close Rolls 2 Edw. II m. 19 d.
2 CONT '''Source:''' Diplomatarium Norvegicum, vol. IXX, no. 463. https://www.dokpro.uio.no/cgi-bin/middelalder/diplom_vise_tekst.cgi?b=16438&s=n&str=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''LITERATURE'''
2 CONT
2 CONT - Heber, Gustav 1944. Norsk adel. https://urn.nb.no/URN:NBN:no-nb_digibok_2018052948501
2 CONT - Martinsen, A. 2026. Vilhjalmsætta : Forslag til et slektstre for lendmennene på Torgar fra 1067 til 1500-tallet, herunder ættene Verdal, Rein, Krøkedans, Bolt, Aspa, Kusse og Hallandsfar samt flere navnløse adelsætter i Trøndela
2 CONC g og Hålogaland Oslo: Forlaget Norsk Adelslitteratur. https://www.lulu.com/shop/a-martinsen/vilhjalms%C3%A6tta/hardcover/product-kvg7w5m.html
2 CONT - Martinsen, Andre VII 2024. But the Godwins Weren't Dead : Supported by Historical and DNA Evidence, this Book Explores How England’s Royal House Came to Norway in 1067 and Has Thousands of Members Living Today ISBN: 978144613612
