1 OCCU djúpúðgu“.Landnámsmaður í Hvammi í Dölum. Var í föruneyti með Auði „, Landnámsmaður í Hvammi í Dölum
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Olaf Feilan Thorsteinsson (Ólaf's faultan Thorsteinsson)=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The nickname bugan means little wolf'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Farmer at Hvammur in Dalabyggð municipality in western Iceland
2 CONT * f. about 870 in Scotland son of Thorstein Red, Earl of Caithness, and his wife Turid Eyvindsdottir.
2 CONT * After his father's death, Olaf was raised by his grandmother Aud Deep-minded and emigrated with her to Iceland, where they settled on the property called Hvamm in the Laxardal region. Olaf married a woman named Alfdis from Barrø
2 CONC  around 920. They got the children Thord Gellir, Ingjald, Grane, Tora, Helga, Thorunn and Thordis
2 CONT * According to the Laxdæla saga, Grandma Aud arranged the engagement and planned the wedding party until the end of the summer (or fall), which she predicted "would be the last party she would hold" she actually died during the fe
2 CONC stival that lasted 3 days, but the party continued to celebrate both Olav's marriage and Aud's death. The landmate book only states that she died during the burial she held for herself pending her own death
2 CONT * Olaf died around 940. Shortly after his death, his nephew Hoskuld Dala-Kollsson named his illegitimate son Olaf the Peacock as namesake[6][7] (Olaf the Peacock had a son Kjartan Óláfsson, who was the beloved of Gudrun Osvifsdott
2 CONC ir, heroine of Laxdæla saga).
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf Feilan Thorsteinsson (Old Norse: Óláfr "feilan" Þorsteinsson, Modern Icelandic: Ólafur "feilan" Þorsteinsson) (c. 890-940) was an Icelandic gothi of the Settlement period. He was the son of Thorstein the Red, jarl of Caithnes
2 CONC s, and his wife Thurid Eyvindsdottir.[1] The byname "feilan" is derived from the Old Irish fáelán, meaning wolfling or little wolf.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his father Olaf was reared by his grandmother Aud the Deep-minded,[2] and emigrated with her to Iceland, where they settled at the estate called Hvamm in the Laxardal region. Olaf married a woman named Alfdis of
2 CONC  Barra,[3] around 920.[4] According to the Laxdæla saga Aud (called "Unn" in the saga) held Olaf dearer than anyone else, and bequested the Hvamm estate to him after her death. She arranged Olaf's betrothal to Alfdis, and planned 
2 CONC the wedding feast for the end of summer (or autumn), which she predicted "would be the last feast I would hold".[5] She indeed died during the festivities that lasted 3-days, but the feast was continued to commemorate both Olaf's 
2 CONC marriage and Aud's death. The Landnámabók gives briefer notice on this, merely stating she died during the funeral feast she held for herself in anticipation of her own death.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =The children of Olaf and Alfdis=
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Thord 
2 CONT * Gellir
2 CONT * Thora
2 CONT * Helga 
2 CONT * Thorunn
2 CONT * Thordis
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' - https://www.revolvy.com/page/Olaf-Feilan
2 CONT
2 CONT =A First Settler of Hvamm in the Dales, From Wikipedia=
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf Feilan ("Olaf little wolf, old nors Ylving, old eng Wulfing, irish name Faelan") (c. 890-940) was an Icelandic gothi of the Settlement period. He was the son of Thorstein the Red, jarl of Caithness, and his wife Thurid Eyvind
2 CONC sdottir. After the death of his father Olaf accompanied his grandmother Aud the Deep-minded to Iceland, and settled with her at Hvamm in the Laxardal region. Around 920 Olaf married a woman named Alfdis. Aud died at their wedding 
2 CONC feast. The children of Olaf and Alfdis were Thord Gellir, Thora, Helga, Thorunn, and Thordis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf died around 940. His nephew Olaf the Peacock was named after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Óláfr erroran Þorsteinsson Name / Precedence: Olaf Feilan Location: Vestl. Island Relations: Son of Thorstein Red. Married to Alfdis from Barrø. Had the sons Ingjald and Grane and their daughters Vigdis, Thordis and Thora. Sources
2 CONC : Iceland Book: 5, Appendix 1, Appendix 2; Landnamabogen: 73, 82, S109, 334 Notes: Also called Óleifr
2 CONT
2 CONT http://heimskringla.no/wiki/Landnamabogen
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olaf_Feilan
2 CONT
