1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Ogmund Sigurdsson Rova, på Hestbø=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ogmund Sigurdsson To Hesby, died January 12, 1311, Accurate year of birth and birthplace is not known; exact date of death and place is not known; mentioned 1295-1311. Knight. Parents: Probably Sigurd Ivarsson (mentioned 1263); 
2 CONC the mother's name is not known. Married to Øyvor Gautesdatter (mentioned 1328), probably the daughter of Baron Gaute Erlingsson to Talgje (dead 1288 or 1289; see NBL1, bd. 4) and a daughter of Gregory Andresson. Father of Finn Ogm
2 CONC undsson (ca. 1290-1343) 
2 CONT * Ogmund Sigurdsson was one of King Håkon 5 Magnusson's most trusted men. Locally connected to Rogaland, he also held a position in the central management. From Ogmund comes the powerful Hesby family on Finnøy in Ryfylke, which ca
2 CONC me to play a prominent role in Norwegian politics and administration in the 14th century.
2 CONT * Ogmund appears for the first time in the sources 1295. He was among the Norwegian guarantors of the armistice between Norway and Denmark, the Hindsgavla Agreement, which ended a several-year state of war between the two countrie
2 CONC s and was renewed during Håkon 5's reign. Ogmund held the title of knight and was ranked No. 17 of the 20 secular Norwegian guarantors. During the division between King Eirik and his younger brother, Duke Håkon, Ogmund acted as th
2 CONC e duke's adviser in Stavanger. In the same period he had a occupation that included Suldal, that is to say at least today's Ryfylke, perhaps the whole of Rogaland.
2 CONT * Around 1300, Ogmund wrote to Hesby (Hestbø) on Finnøy in Ryfylke. Hesby was probably the main farm in a royal estate complex that Ogmund had laid out as a road or service estate. Although it is not proven that Ogmund wrote to He
2 CONC sby after 1302, he must have kept the estate, because both his son Finn and his son Ogmund resided there.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married  '''Eyvor /Øyvor Gautesdatter Talgje,''' daughter of Baron Gaute Erlingsen Talgje and Sigrid Gregoriusdatter of Stavreim. (Øyvor Gautesdatter was born about 1268 and died around 1350 in Nes, Sauherad, Telemark.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children =
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Ivar Ogmundsson''' (born no later than 1290-1349); married Kristin Toresdatter Sørum and Ronnaug Smidrsdatter?, grandfather to Ogmund Finnsson (ca. 1321-1388).
2 CONT * '''Finn Ogmundsson,''' died † 1343, he was knights and national council, married Gudrun Sæbjørnsdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.ub.uit.no/ansatte/jorune/Avhandlingrevidert.pdf History]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogmund Sigurdsson was one of King Håkon 5 Magnusson's most trusted men. Locally linked to Rogaland, he also had a position in the central government. From Ogmund, the mighty Hesby-set originates at Finnøy in Ryfylke, which came to
2 CONC  play a prominent role in Norwegian politics and administration in the 14th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogmund was probably the son of the Sigurd, son of Ivar Rova, who was among the selected crew on Håkon 4 Håkonssons ship under the west sea train in 1263. The location on the king's ship suggests that the family had all then a prom
2 CONC inent position. Ogmund's wife was probably of the Talgje family, because grandson Ogmund Finnsson later owned Talgje as heritage. According to several researchers, among others Gustav Storm, Asgaut Steinnes and Halvard Bjørkvik, w
2 CONC ere Ogmund Sigurdsson married to Mrs Øyvor Gautesdatter, who may have been the daughter of Baron Gaute Erlingsson to Talgje and possibly daughter daughter of Håkon Håkonsson's daughter Cecilia. If so, Ogmund was besieged with the 
2 CONC royal family. (Genoallogist Lars Løberg believes that Ogund's wife was another daughter of Gaute that we do not know the name of.) Ogmund's sons Finn and Ivar, both knights and national councils, were among the country's leading m
2 CONC en in the first half of the 14th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogmund first appeared in the sources in 1295. He was among the Norwegian guarantors of the truce between Norway and Denmark, the Hindsgavlav speech, which ended a several years of war between the two countries and was renewed into
2 CONC  Håkon 5's reign. Ogmund led the district court and was ranked No. 17 of the 20 worldly Norwegian guarantors. During the division between King Eirik and his younger brother, Duke Håkon, Ogmund appeared as the duke's advisor in Sta
2 CONC vanger. During the same period he had an office that included Suldal, that would say at least today's Ryfylke, perhaps all of Rogaland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1300 Ogmund wrote to Hesby (Hestbø) on Finnøy in Ryfylke. Hesby was probably the main farm in a royal estate complex which Ogmund got laid out as a grocery or service. Even though it was not stated that Ogmund signed up to 
2 CONC Hesby after 1302, he must have kept the goods, for both his son Finn and his son Ogmund resided there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ogmund appears to have been named as a marking man at the latest when Duke Håkon joined the reigning king in the summer of 1299. He is the first time to be covered by the title in a letter that was probably issued shortly after De
2 CONC cember 1 of the same year. As a markman, Ogmund had one of the highest offices in Håkon 5's hird and led the inner courtroom there. Håkon also gave the markman tasks as the king's lawyer and who oversees the court administration, 
2 CONC but whether this happened in Ogmund's lifetime is uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also as a royal adviser, the brand man gained a stronger position during this period. This was expressed in, among other things, Håkon 5's provisions 1302 on a possible future guardian rule for a minor king. Of a total of 12 membe
2 CONC rs in the guardian government, "the council of the kingdom", the chancellor, co-owner and two others were to sit permanently together and fire the daily rule of the state. The fact that the King 1308 appointed a co-keeper in addit
2 CONC ion to Ogmund, expressly without reducing the position and rank of the latter, testifies to the same. The appointment was probably due to the need to have one brand name in the North African country and one in the Norwegian-born N
2 CONC orway. Ogmund is at least not covered outside Western Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1309 and 1311, Ogmund Sigurdsson was co-hosted by a witness letter in Bergen about Bishop Arne's initiative to get the German winter seats there to pay tithing. In the last letter, dated January 12, 1311, he titled Baron; Perha
2 CONC ps he was the last in Norway to receive such rank. Ogmund probably died shortly after this.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Events=
2 CONT
2 CONT • He lived in periods at Hesby, Finnøy. He stayed here while he was a Governor of Ryfylke, but also later had Hesby as a seat, even though he probably stayed a lot in Bergen while he was the King's adviser.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source - Avhandlingrevidert, Page 402 
2 CONT
