2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/90/cf/15/65/534448656ad6104c/a_viking_2024_original.jpg?hash=7ba2e6b13b789702719671930e5e3c51030f82b4cb45a106adf70eb68bb149af.1797753599
2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE Viking Encampment:
3 CONT
3 CONT March is women’s history month! To celebrate, we will be highlighting female historical figures who had a major impact on the history of the Viking age. Today's highlight is on one of the lesser talked about women of Viking societ
3 CONC y, Hallgerður Höskuldsdóttir. Hallgerður is said to have been born in 940 A.D. Iceland and is a major instigator in the feud between Gunnar Hámundarson and Njáll Þorgeirsson in the Icelandic epic Njál’s Saga. As a child, it was sa
3 CONC id that Hallgerður’s beauty would cause many to suffer. As an adult, Hallgerður is described as having flowing long hair and was very tall earning her the nickname “longbrók” (long pants). She was married three times with the last
3 CONC  marriage to the chieftain Gunnar Hámundarson. As the wife of a powerful chieftain, Hallgerður held a great deal of power over the region and she wanted to expand her family’s power. To this end, Hallgerður began insulting Gunnar’
3 CONC s friend and rival chieftain Njáll in an attempt to weaken his standing in the community. This led to Njáll’s wife, Bergþóra Skarphéðinsdottir, coming to her husband’s defense and insulting Gunnar. The insults then escalated into 
3 CONC both women ordering killings against the rival families in an attempt to spur their husbands to seize power from the other. Gunnar and Njáll settled the killings with compensation but Hallgerður continued to feud against Njáll eve
3 CONC n after Gunnar’s death. While not a flashy warrior or all-powerful queen, Hallgerður represents the means that common, free, landholding women could use to exercise power in Viking society. While men were the typical rulers in the
3 CONC  early medieval patriarchal society, women were able to exert their influence from behind the scene, often in spite of their husband’s intentions.
3 CONT
3 CONT Pictured: A 19th century artistic depiction of Hallgerður and Gunnar meeting at the Althing 
