2 CONT '''Kjarval konge (Kjarvalr konungr)''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Cerball mac Dúnlainge konge av Osraige, Irland fra 842 til 888'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * f. ca 800 i Osraige, Cerball var sønn av Dúngal mac Fergaile og ukjent mor 
2 CONT * Han var gift med Maelfelbha, de hadde barna Diarmait, Cellach, Bráenán,  Rafarta[,  Gormlaith,  Dunghal ,  Eithne,  Cuilde, Mór og  Fridgerd
2 CONT
2 CONT Han tok over etter sin far og  var en av Osraiges mest fremragende konger. Han regjerte i en turbulent periode i irsk historie og med  angrep fra vikingene Cerballs første kamp mot vikingene ble rapportert i 846.Han ble også med i
2 CONC  alle vikingegruppene da det passet hans politikk. Han ledet Osraige som ble  fremtredende i Irlands politikk, og han blir  selv militært dominerende over Leath Moga. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kjarvalr konungr Tilnavn / fordanskning: Kjarval Lokalitet: Dublin, Irland Relationer: Havde sønnen Dufnjal og døtrene Fridgerd, Rafarta og Kormlød. Kilder: Landnamabogen: 2, 175, S217, 321, 348  Noter: Irsk konge, som herskede ov
2 CONC er Dublin. Ifølge Hermann Pálsson er denne Kjarval identisk med Cerbhall af Osraige (d. 888), som dog aldrig herskede over Dublin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://heimskringla.no/wiki/Landnamabogen
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerball_mac_D%C3%BAnlainge
2 CONT
2 CONT '''TRANSLATION: Cerball mac Dúnlainge King of Osraige, Ireland from 842 to 888'''
2 CONT
2 CONT b. about 800 in Osraige, Cerball was the son of Dúngal mac Fergaile and unknown mother
2 CONT He was married to Maelfelbha, they had the children Diarmait, Cellach, Bráenán, Rafarta [, Gormlaith, Dunghal, Eithne, Cuilde, Mór and Fridgerd
2 CONT He took over from his father and was one of Osraige's most outstanding kings. He ruled during a turbulent period in Irish history and with attacks from the Vikings Cerball's first battle against the Vikings was reported in 846. He
2 CONC  also joined all the Viking groups when it suited his policy. He led Osraige, who became prominent in Irish politics, and he himself becomes militarily dominant over Leath Moga.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kjarvalr konungr Nickname / pre-Danish: Kjarval Locality: Dublin, Ireland Relationships: Had son Dufnjal and daughters Fridgerd, Rafarta and Kormlød. Sources: Landnamabogen: 2, 175, S217, 321, 348 Notes: Irish king who ruled over 
2 CONC Dublin. According to Hermann Pálsson, this Kjarval is identical with Cerbhall of Osraige (d. 888), who, however, never ruled over Dublin.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://heimskringla.no/wiki/Landnamabogen
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT CERBALL MAC DÚNLAINGE (d. 888)
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of Cerball mac Dúnlainge (842–88) the Osraige rose from relative obscurity to become a major player in Irish politics. The most lavish account of his deeds survives in an eleventh-century saga embedded in The Frag
2 CONC mentary Annals of Ireland. This saga appears to have been written under the sponsorship of Cerball’s great-great grandson Donnchad mac GillaPátraic, who ruled Osraige (1003–1039) and Leinster (1033–1039). The exaggerations and ana
2 CONC chronisms found in the saga urge a degree of caution in its use as a historical source. Cerball is also mentioned in the Icelandic Landnámabók and later sagas. These demonstrate that a number of prominent Icelandic families claime
2 CONC d descent from Cerball as a figure of legend.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cerball’s kingdom, Osraige, was strategically placed between the heartlands of Munster and Leinster. At the beginning of Cerball’s reign, Osraige owed allegiance to overkings of Munster. However, in the 850s and 860s the fortunes 
2 CONC of Munster declined, a factor that can be seen to aid Cerball’s advancement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, Cerball also faced dangers from Viking incursions. He is most renowned for his victories over Vikings that are elaborated in The Fragmentary Annals of Ireland. Cerball’s first battle against Vikings is reported in 84
2 CONC 6. He also allied with some Viking groups when it suited his policies. In the late 850s he joined forces with Ívarr, a king of the “Dark foreigners.” In 859, they raided Southern Uí Néill, thus challenging the power of the Uí Néil
2 CONC l overking Máel-Sechnaill mac Máele-Ruanaid. In consequence of this attack, a royal meeting was arranged at Rathugh (Co. Westmeath) in 859. Osraige was formally ceded from Munster control and placed under the authority of Máel-Sec
2 CONC hnaill. The event caused Cerball to reject his alliance with Ívarr. Further hostilities against Vikings are recorded for the remainder of Cerball’s reign, although a temporary alliance with one viking group is recorded in 864.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cerball was able to deal effectively with the threats posed by other Irish kings. Cerball enjoyed good relations with the Loígis of Leinster (his sister Lann was initially married to the king of this population group). Nevertheles
2 CONC s, Cerball engaged in hostilities against other kings in the province on at least three occasions. The marriages of Cerball’s daughters to kings of Uí Cheinnselaig and Uí Dróna in Leinster may indicate attempts to reduce border wa
2 CONC rfare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cerball’s relations with Munster fluctuated. In 864, he attacked the heartlands of the province. He later allied with Dúnchad mac Duibdábairenn, who became overking of Munster in 872. They plundered Connacht together in 871 and 87
2 CONC 3. His alliance with Dúnchad later collapsed, and Cerball campaigned in Munster in 878.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cerball’s relations with the powerful Uí Néill rulers seem to have been flexible and pragmatic. From 859, Cerball supported Máel-Sechnaill against his rival Áed, overking of Northern Uí Néill. Nevertheless, Cerball quickly joined 
2 CONC sides with Áed following Máel-Sechnaill’s death. Cerball’s sister Lann assisted in securing these important alliances by marrying both kings in succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cerball ruled for a total of forty-six years. His longevity, success, and the dramatic potential of events in his career encouraged the later development of legends about him.
2 CONT
2 CONT CLARE DOWNHAM
2 CONT
2 CONT References and Further Reading
2 CONT
2 CONT Clarke, Howard B. et al., eds. Ireland and Scandinavia in the Early Viking Age. Dublin: Four Courts Press, 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ó Corráin, Donnchadh. “Nationality and Kingship in Pre-Norman Ireland.” In Nationality and the Pursuit of National Independence, Historical Studies 11, edited by T.W.Moody, 1–35. Belfast: Blackstaff Press, 1978.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ó Corráin, Donnchadh. “Viking Ireland: Afterthoughts.” In H.B. Clarke et al. eds. Ireland and Scandinavia in the Early Viking Age, edited by H.B.Clarke et al., 442–445, 447. Dublin: Four Courts Press, 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT Radner, Joan Newlon, ed. and trans. Fragmentary Annals of Ireland. Dublin: Dublin Institute for Advanced Studies, 1978.
2 CONT
2 CONT Radner, Joan N. “Writing History: Early Irish Historiography and the Significance of Form.” Celtica 23 (1999):312–325.
2 CONT
