1 OCCU Mørejarl, Earl of Møre and Romsdal, Earl of More and Romsdal, Earl of More, also "The Wise", Earl of Shetland and Orkney, Count of Maer, Jarl of North and South More and Of Ramsdal, Jarl á Mæri., Jarl á Mæri, EARL OF MORE, Earl, J
2 CONC arl
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rǫgnvaldr Mörejarl, son Eysteins glumru. Earl of Møre and father of Gange-Hrolf Rollo of Normandie.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Basics ==
2 CONT * Father: Eystein "Glumra" Ivarsson
2 CONT * Mother: Asdis (Ascrida) (undocumented)
2 CONT * Concubines: Names unknown
2 CONT ** Son: Hallad
2 CONT ** Son: Hrollaug, who settled in Iceland
2 CONT ** Son: Torf-Einar, who settled in Orkney
2 CONT * Wife: Ragnhild (also called Hild) Hrolfsdatter
2 CONT ** Son: Gange-Rolf, who settled in Normandie
2 CONT ** Son: Ivar, killed in battle
2 CONT ** Son: Tore Teiande, who became a jarl in Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT The idea that his mother was named "Aseda" has been traced back to "The mammoth book of British kings and queens" by Mike Ashley, published 1999. So far, no evidence of what he based that theory on.
2 CONT
2 CONT == LInks and notes ==
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORWEGIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#Ragnvalddied894A http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ragnvald_M%C3%B8rejarl http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ragnvald_Eysteinsson http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=&emne=nor&person=Rag
2 CONC nvald%20%D8ysteinsson
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Død omkring 892. Han var sønn av Øystein Glumra.  Ragnvald var gift med Hild Rolvsdatter. De hadde barna: 1. Gange-Rolv Ragnvaldson av Normandie. Født mellom 860 og 866. Død 931. 2. Tore Teiande Ragnvaldson Mørejarl. Født omkring 
2 CONC 862. Død mellom 938 og 940.
2 CONT
2 CONT I henhold til Orknøyingenes saga går Ragnvalds ætt tilbake til «Heite Gors sønn var far til Sveide sjøkonge, far til Halvdan den gamle, far til Ivar Opplendingejarl, far til Øystein Glumra, far til Ragnvald jarl den mektige og den
2 CONC  rådsnare». Den samme sagaen strekker hans ætt tilbake til en mytologisk opprinnelse til skikkelse ved navn Fornjot fra Finland eller Kvenland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnvald levde på Harald Hårfagres tid, og ble av ham forlenet med Nordmøre, Romsdal og Sundmøre i 865. Han var av sine samtidige høyt ansett for sin klokskap og ble kalt «den mektige». Det var han som klipte Haralds hår, som da i
2 CONC kke var klippet på 10 år.
2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT RAGNVALD "the Wise", son of [EYSTEIN "Glumra/Clatterer" Jarl in Norway & his wife ---] (-[894]).  Orkneyinga Saga names “Eystein the Clatterer, father of the wise counsellor Earl Rognwald the Powerful…”, adding that “Earl Rognwald
2 CONC  campaigned with King Harald Fine-Hair who gave him charge of North More, South More and Romsdale”[141].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "Ragnvald Earl of More, a son of Eystein "Glumra" when recording that he had become a supporter of King Harald who had invested him with the districts of North More and Raumsdal[142].  Snorre records that he was creat
2 CONC ed Jarl of North and South Möre and of Raumsdal in Norway by Harald I "Hårfagre" King of Norway after his victory at Solskiel [869] against Hunthiof King of Möre and Nokve King of Raumsdal[143].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga and Snorre both record that King Harald granted Shetland and Orkney to Ragnvald in compensation for the death of his son Ivar[144].  The Historia Norwegie records that "principi Rogwaldi" crossed the Solund Sea, de
2 CONC stroyed the peoples of the Orkney islands, in the days of "Haraldi Comati regis…Norwegie"[145].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that “Halfdan Long-Leg and Gudrod Gleam, King Harald´s sons by Snæfrid” attacked “Earl Rognvald of More, killed him and assumed his authority”[146].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre records that Ragnvald was ambushed in his hall and burned alive by Halfdan Haaleg and Gudred Liomi, two of King Harald's sons[147]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m [RAGNHILD, daughter of HROLF “Nevja/Nose” & his wife ---.  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earl Rognwald” married “Ragnhild the daughter of Hrolf Nose”[148].  Snorre names "Hild, a daughter of Rolf Nefia" as the wife of "Earl Ragn
2 CONC vald"[149].] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [Ragnvald & his wife had three children:] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.             [IVAR (-killed in battle either Hafrsfiord [872] or Orkney [874]).  Orkneyinga Saga names “Ivar and Thorer the Silent” as the two other sons of “Earl Rognwald” and his wife “Ragnhild the daughter of Hrolf Nose”, add
2 CONC ing that Ivar was killed in battle fighting with Harald I "Hårfagre" King of Norway in Scotland[150].  Snorre names "Ivar, a son of Ragnvald Earl of More" when recording his death in battle during a Viking campaign against the Sco
2 CONC ttish islands[151].  The Complete Peerage dates the appointment of Sigurd (Ivar´s reported paternal uncle) as Jarl of Orkney to [875][152], which means that Ivar must have been killed shortly before this date.  However, as explain
2 CONC ed below this causes considerable chronological difficulties with the reported events in the career of Turf-Einar, Ivar´s youngest illegitimate half-brother, so should be considered as extremely approximate.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.             [ROLLO [Hrolf "Ganger/the Walker"] (-[928]).  Orkneyinga Saga names “Hrolf who conquered Normandy” as son of “Earl Rognwald” and his wife “Ragnhild the daughter of Hrolf Nose”, adding that he was so big that no hors
2 CONC e could carry him, giving rise to his name “Göngu-Hrolf”[153].  Snorre names "Rolf and Thorer" as the two sons of "Earl Ragnvald" and his wife Hild, recording that Rolf was banished from Norway by King Harald and travelled to the 
2 CONC Hebrides, settling first in Orkney before moving southwards through Scotland, and eventually conquering Normandy[154].  The Historia Norwegie records that, after Orkney was conquered by "principi Rogwaldi" and his followers, "de q
2 CONC uorum collegio…Rodulfus" captured Rouen in Normandy, commenting that he was known as "Gongurolfr" because he was obliged to walk as he was too large to travel on horseback[155].  This source makes no reference to any blood relatio
2 CONC nship between Rollo and "principi Rogwaldi".  According to Dudo of Saint-Quentin, Rollo arrived in northern France in 876[156], although there is some debate about [900] being a more likely date[157].  William of Jumièges records 
2 CONC that Rollo was chosen by lot to be leader of the Viking colonists[158].  Viking raids intensified in northern France.  Although they were defeated after raiding Chartres [911], Charles III "le Simple" King of the West Franks grant
2 CONC ed the Normans land around Rouen in which to settle[159].  The uncertain nature of the demise was the source of future problems between the French crown, which claimed that it was an enfeofment for which the ruler owed allegiance,
2 CONC  and the later Dukes of Normandy who claimed it was an unconditional allod for which no allegiance was owed.  A charter dated 14 Mar 918 which granted land to the monastery of Saint-Germain-des-Prés "except that part…which we have
2 CONC  granted to the Normans of the Seine, namely to Rollo and his companions"[160].  He was later known as ROBERT I Comte [de Normandie].]    
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.             [THORE Ragnvaldsson "Tause/the Silent".  Orkneyinga Saga names “Ivar and Thorer the Silent” as the two other sons of “Earl Rognwald” and his wife “Ragnhild the daughter of Hrolf Nose”[161].  Snorre names "Rolf and T
2 CONC horer" as the two sons of "Earl Ragnvald" and his wife Hild[162].  He succeeded his father in [894] as Jarl of Möre, having dispossessed Gudrod "Ljome", son of King Harald, who had seized Möre on the death of Jarl Ragnvald[163].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([890]) ALOF "Aarbod/Season-bettering", daughter of HARALD I "Hårfagre/Harfagri/Fairhair" King of Norway & his second wife Gyda of Hordaland.  Snorre records that King Harald gave Jarl Thore his daughter "Alof, called Arbot" aft
2 CONC er the king confirmed him as Jarl of Möre[164].]  [Thore & his wife had one child:] 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)             [BERGLJOT Thoresdatter.  The Historia Norwegie names "Bergliota filia Thoris Tacentis", from "nobilissima Morensium ac Halogensium comitum prosapia", as the wife of "Siwardo"[165].  Snorre names "Bergljot, a daughte
2 CONC r of Earl Thorer the Silent" & his wife as the wife of Sigurd[166].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m SIGURD Jarl,  son of HAAKON Grjotgardson Jarl of Haalogaland & his wife -- (-murdered Oglo 962).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [Ragnvald had three illegitimate children by unknown mistresses:] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.              [HALLAD .  Orkneyinga Saga names “Hallad, Hrollaug and the youngest Einar” as “natural sons” of “Earl Rognwald”[167].  Snorre names "Hallad, the second Einar, the third Hrollaug" as the three sons of "Earl Ragnvald
2 CONC " by concubines, adding that they were all "grown men" when their legitimate brothers were still children[168].  Orkneyinga Saga records that Ragnvald sent “his son Hallad west to the islands” after hearing of the death of his bro
2 CONC ther and nephew, and that Harald I "Hårfagre" King of Norway gave “the title of earl” to Hallad who “came to Orkney and took up residence on Mainland”[169].  Snorre records that Earl Ragnvald installed his son Hallad as Jarl of Or
2 CONC kney after the death of his brother Sigurd, but that he resigned the earldom and returned to Norway[170].  Orkneyinga Saga records that, following complaints by farmers about Viking raids, Hallad “tiring of his rule, gave up the e
2 CONC arldom and went back to Norway as a common landholder” which “made him a laughing stock”[171]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.              [HROLLAUG .  Orkneyinga Saga names “Hallad, Hrollaug and the youngest Einar” as “natural sons” of “Earl Rognwald”[172].  Snorre names "Hallad, the second Einar, the third Hrollaug" as the three sons of "Earl Ragnva
2 CONC ld" by concubines, adding that they were all "grown men" when their legitimate brothers were still children[173].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that Ragnvald told his son Hrollaug that “your fate will take you to Iceland [where] you´ll have plenty of descendants”[174].  His alleged descendants in Iceland shown below are as stated in Thorstein Side-
2 CONC Hallum's Saga and Flatey-book's St Olaf's Saga[175] but the accuracy of this information, which has not been corroborated in other sources, is unknown.  m ---.  The name of Hrollaug´s wife is not known.]  [Hrollaug & his wife had 
2 CONC one child:]
2 CONT
2 CONT a)              [JON Ozur .  m ---.  The name of Jon´s wife is not known.]  [Jon & his wife had one child:] 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)               [THOREY .  m ---.  The name of Thorey´s wife is not known.]  [Thorey & his wife had one child:] 
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)            [HALL of Sida .  m ---.  The name of Hall´s wife is not known.]  [Hall & his wife had two children:]
2 CONT
2 CONT (1)            [THORSTEIN .  Aged 20 at the battle of Clontarf 1014.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2)            [THORDIS .  Orkneyinga Saga records that “the mother of…Ospak was Thordis, daughter of Hall of Sida”[176].  m ---.  The name of Thordis´s husband is not known.]  Thordis & her husband had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a.              USPAK .  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earl Erlend Thorfinnson” married “Thora, the daughter of Sumarlidi Ospaksson”, adding that “the mother of…Ospak was Thordis, daughter of Hall of Sida”[177].  m ---.  The name 
2 CONC of Uspak´s wife is not known.  Uspak & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT (i)              SOMERLED .  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earl Erlend Thorfinnson” married “Thora, the daughter of Sumarlidi Ospaksson”[178].  m ---.  The name of Somerled´s wife is not known.  Somerled & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)           THORA .  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earl Erlend Thorfinnson” married “Thora, the daughter of Sumarlidi Ospaksson”, adding that “the mother of this Ospak was Thordis, daughter of Hall of Sida”[179].  Orkneyinga Sag
2 CONC a records that “his mother was married…to a man called Sigurd” when her son Magnus returned to Orkney, adding that she and her second husband had a son “Hakon Karl…[and] a farm at Paplay”[180].  m firstly ERLEND Thorfinsson Jarl o
2 CONC f Orkney, son of THORFINN "the Black" Jarl of Orkney & his wife Ingibjörg Finnsdatter (-in prison Nidaros 1098, bur Nidaros).  m secondly SIGURD of Paplay . 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)              [HALLBERA .  m ASBIORN, son of HRAFUNKEL of Steinrodar-Stadir in Iceland.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.              [EINAR "Turf-Einar” .  Orkneyinga Saga names “Hallad, Hrollaug and the youngest Einar” as “natural sons” of “Earl Rognwald”[181].  Snorre names "Hallad, the second Einar, the third Hrollaug" as the three sons of "E
2 CONC arl Ragnvald" by concubines, adding that they were all "grown men" when their legitimate brothers were still children[182].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that Ragnvald sent his “youngest son Einar” to “the islands” after his brother Hallad returned to Norway and that Harald I "Hårfagre" King of Norway gave “the title of earl”, adding that his mother was “sla
2 CONC ve-born on each side of her family”, and stating that he killed “Thorir Tree-Beard and Kalf Scurvy” and succeeded in imposing his authority in Orkney where “he was the first man to dig peat for fuel…at Tarbat Ness in Scotland”, an
2 CONC d that he “was tall and ugly…and…one-eyed”[183].  It should be noted that there are considerable chronological difficulties with the career of Turf-Einar and his sons, as reported in Orkneyinga Saga.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The starting point for the analysis of these problems is the date of the battle in Northumbria in which Turf-Einar´s two oldest sons were killed, which can with reasonable accuracy be placed in the early 950s.  If these two sons w
2 CONC ere at least in their late thirties or forties when they died (a difficult assumption to make considering that they had no reported direct heirs), their births could not be placed before [905/15] at the earliest.  The likelihood w
2 CONC ould then be that their father, Turf-Einar, was not born much earlier than [975/95].  The difficulty is that this date is completely incompatible with (a) the fact that the illegitimate sons of Ragnvald were reported as “grown men
2 CONC ” when their legitimate half-brothers were children, and (b) that the date when Ivar, the oldest legitimate son, was killed is estimated to [874].  
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT Snorre Sturlason: Harald Hårfagres saga: «10. ... Ragnvald Mørejarl, sønn til Øystein Glumra, var blitt kong Haralds mann da om sommeren. Kong Harald satte ham til høvding over disse to fylkene, Nordmøre og Romsdal, og ga ham rett
2 CONC  til hjelp både av stormenn og bønder, likeså skip nok til å verge landet for ufred. Han ble kalt Ragnvald jarl den mektige eller den rådsnare, og de sier at begge navnene var sanne. Kong Harald var i Trondheimen vinteren som fulg
2 CONC te.»
2 CONT
2 CONT «23. Kong Harald var i gjestebud på Møre hos Ragnvald jarl; da hadde han lagt hele landet under seg. Da gikk kongen i bad der. Og nå lot kong Harald håret sitt greie, og Ragnvald jarl skar håret hans; da hadde det ikke vært skåret
2 CONC  eller kjemmet på ti år. Før kalte de ham Harald Luva, men nå ga Ragnvald jarl ham nytt navn, og kalte ham Harald Hårfagre. Alle som så ham, sa at det var virkelig et sant navn, for han hadde et hår som var både stort og vakkert.»
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnvald ble innebrent på sin gård sammen med 60 mann av Halvdan Hålegg og Gudrød Ljome, to av Haralds sønner, som vill være jarler over Møre. 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1). Snorre Sturlason: Harald Hårfagres saga, avsnitt 10, 23-24. Snorre Sturlason: Olav den helliges saga, avsnitt 96. Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 278. Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 50, 76. 
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------
2 CONT Ragnvald's life is described by Snorri Sturluson in The Saga of Harald Hårfagre (Fairhair):  http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=no&emne=asatru&person=&list=&vis=s_e_harald_harfagre#43
2 CONT ---------------------------------------
2 CONT About the name Ragnvald in various versions: http://www.nordicnames.de/pojk_r/Ragnvald.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ragnvald Øysteinsson Mørejarl (norrønt Rögnvaldr Mœrajarl) (circa 830 - 892) var en jarl på nordvestlandet i Norge på midten av 800-tallet, tilsvarende det området som i dag heter Møre og Romsdal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnvalds ætt Ragnvald var sønn av Øystein Ivarsson som ble kalt for Øystein Glumra. I henhold til Orknøyingenes saga går Ragnvalds ætt tilbake til «Heite Gors sønn var far til Sveide sjøkonge, far til Halvdan den gamle, far til I
2 CONC var Opplendingejarl, far til Øystein Glumra, far til Ragnvald jarl den mektige og den rådsnare». Den samme sagaen strekker hans ætt tilbake til en mytologisk opprinnelse til skikkelse ved navn Fornjot fra Finland eller Kvenland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre Sturlasson skriver i Harald Hårfagres saga i Heimskringla: «Ragnvald Mørejarl var den kjæreste venn kong Harald hadde, og kongen satte ham høyt. Ragnvald jarl var gift med Hild, datter til Rolv Nevja; deres sønner var Rolv 
2 CONC og Tore. Ragnvald jarl hadde noen frillesønner også; en het Hallad; en annen Einar og en tredje Rollaug; de var voksne da de ektefødte brødrene deres var barn ennå. Rolv var en stor viking; han var så svær til vekst at ingen hest 
2 CONC kunne bære ham, derfor gikk han til fots overalt. Han ble kalt for Gange-Rolv. Han herjet ofte i austerveg.»
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnvald var således far til sagnomsuste Gange-Rolf som i sagalitteraturen er blitt identifisert som den nordbo som franskmennene kaller for «Rollo» og som grunnla et jarldømme i Normandie. Dette har imidlertid aldri blitt histori
2 CONC sk dokumentert. Via sønnen Torv-Einar ble Ragnvalds ætt giftet inn i det skotske kongehuset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den rådsnare Ifølge Snorre satte Harald Hårfagre Ragnvald til å være jarl over Nordmøre og Romsdal (og etter hvert Sunnmøre). Kongen gav ham rett til hjelp av både stormenn og bønder, likeså skip nok til å verge landet for ufred. 
2 CONC Ragnvald ble også kalt Ragnvald jarl den mektige eller den rådsnare, og det ble sagt at begge navnene var sanne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kongen skal ha betraktet Ragnvald som en av sine viktigste menn og legenden sier at Ragnvald var den som klippet kongens hår da han etter ti år var blitt konge over alle i Norge. Før ble kongen kalt for «Harald Luva» (Lurvehode), 
2 CONC men etter at håret var skåret, ga Ragnvald ham et nytt navn, «Hårfagre».
2 CONT
2 CONT Orknøyene Snorre forteller at Ragnvald sendte sønnen Hallad vestover etter at broren Sigurd var blitt drept, men han ble så plaget av vikinger og ransmenn at han ble lei og kom tilbake. Faren ble da sint og skjelte ham ut for ikke
2 CONC  å ligne på forfedrene sine. Den andre sønnen, Einar, lovte da at han kunne reise til Orknøyene og aldri komme tilbake til Norge mer. Ragnvald jarl skal da ha sagt at det var like greit om han aldri kom tilbake igjen: «For jeg har
2 CONC  ikke større håp om at dine frender får ære av deg, for alle i morsætta di er trellbårne».
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnvald utstyrte sønnen med skip og mannskap og lot ham dro vestover hvor han kom i kamp med vikinger og drepte dem. På Orknøyene ble han kalt for Torv-Einar for han lot skjære torv som brensel for det vokste ikke trær der. Han b
2 CONC le jarl på Orknøyene og en mektig mann, enøyd og stygg å se på.
2 CONT
2 CONT Innebrent Da Harald Hårfagre tok til å eldes ble sønnene hans Halvdan Hålegg og Gudrød Ljome stadig mer misfornøyd med at de selv ikke hadde noe rike mens faren hadde innsatt jarler rundt om i fylkene. De besluttet da å ikke vente
2 CONC  mer på odelen sin. Snorre skriver at «de dro ut med en stor flokk og kom uventet over Ragnvald Mørejarl, kringsatte huset hans og brente ham inne med seksti mann.»
2 CONT
2 CONT Da kongen hørte dette dro han med en stor hær mot Gudrød som overga seg og ble sendt til Agder. Kongen innsatte så Tore Teiande, sønn av Ragnvald Mørejarl, som ny jarl og giftet ham med datteren Ålov. Kongsdatteren ble siden kalt 
2 CONC «Årbot». Halvdan Hålegg dro derimot over til Orknøyene, kom uventet på Torv-Einar som måtte flykte, men samme høst kom Torv-Einar tilbake og overrasket Halvdan og fikk ham drept.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Original text: Snorri, Heimskringla, Haralds saga hins hárfagra: "Rögnvaldr Mœrajarl, son Eysteins glumru, hafði þá um sumarit gerzt maðr Haralds konungs. Haraldr konungr setti hann höfðingja yfir þessi tvau fylki, Norðmœri ok Rau
2 CONC msdal, ok fékk honum þar styrk til bæði af ríkismönnum ok bóndum, svá ok skipakost at verja landit fyrir úfriði. Hann var kallaðr Rögnvaldr hinn ríki eða hinn ráðsvinni, ok segja menn at hvárrtveggja væri sannnefni. Haraldr konung
2 CONC r var um vetrinn eptir í Þrándheimi."
2 CONT
2 CONT [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT "12. Brenna Vemundar konungs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eptir þat lagði Haraldr konungr undir sik Sunnmœri. Vemundr, bróðir Auðbjarnar konungs, hélt Firðafylki ok gerðist konungr yfir. Þetta var síðla um haust, ok gera menn þat ráð með Haraldi konungi, at hann skyldi eigi fara suðr um 
2 CONC Stað á haustdegi. Þá setti Haraldr konungr Rögnvald jarl yfir Mœri hváratveggju ok Raumsdal, ok hafði hann þá um sik mikit fjölmenni. Haraldr konungr sneri þá norðr aptr til Þrándheims. Þann sama vetr fór Rögnvaldr jarl hit iðra u
2 CONC m Eið, ok svá suðr um Fjörðu. Hann hafði njósn af Vemundi konungi, ok kom um nótt þar sem heitir Naustdalr; Var Vemundr konungr þar á veizlu. Rögnvaldr jarl tók hús á þeim, ok brendi konung inni með níutigi manna. Eptir þat kom Be
2 CONC rðlukári til Rögnvalds jarls með langskip alskipat, ok fóru þeir báðir norðr á Mœri. Tók Rögnvaldr jarl skip þau, er átt hafði Vemundr konungr, ok alt lausafé þat er hann fékk. Berðlukári fór norðr til Þrándheims á fund Haralds ko
2 CONC nungs ok gerðist hans maðr; hann var berserkr mikill."
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ragnvald Eysteinsson, The Wise (830-890), Earl of Sunnmøre, Nordmøre and Romsdal, was born in Maer Nord-Trøndelag, Norway and died at the Orkney Islands.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was son of Eystein Glumra the Noisy, Earl of Oppland, and grandson of Halfdan the Old.
2 CONT
2 CONT His second wife was Ragnhild Ragnhild Hrolfsdottir (Raghldr (Hldr) Hrolfsdóttir) daughter of Hrolfr Nefjaa. Ragnvald was the father of Hrolf Ganger, the founder of Normandy. He was also the father of Turf-Einar, the ancestor of th
2 CONC e jarls of Orkney.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the direct ancestor to William I of England, Edward III of England, James I of England, and, therefore, Elizabeth II of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland. He is therefore the ancestor of most of the r
2 CONC oyal families of Europe.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ragnvald Eystemssön, allmänt kallad Mörejarl. norsk storman i 9:de årh., åtnjöt i hög grad Harald Hårfagres förtroende och styrde såsom hans jarl Mörerne (Nord- och Söndmöre samt Romsdalen). R. blef genom sin ene son. Torv-Einar, 
2 CONC stamfader för Orkenö-jarlarna och genom den andre, Gånge-Rolf, stamfader för hertigarna af Normandie. Af sina samtida kallades han den »rådmilde» och den »mäktige». Trots sin ådagalagda trohet mot konungen överfölls och dräptes ha
2 CONC n af dennes söner omkr. 890. Y. s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Från Nordisk Familjebok
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rognvald "The Wise" Eysteinsson (son of Eystein Ivarsson) is the founder of the Earldom of Orkney in the Norse Sagas. Three quite different accounts of the creation of the Norse earldom on Orkney and Shetland exist. The best known
2 CONC  is that found in the Heimskringla, but other older traditions are found in the Historia Norvegiae and the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The saga accounts are the best known, and the latest, of the three surviving traditions concerning Rognvald and the foundation of the Earldom of Orkney. Recorded in the 13th century, their views are informed by Norwegian politics 
2 CONC of the day. Once, historians could write that no-one denied the reality of Harald Fairhair's expeditions to the west recounted in Heimskringla, but this is no longer the case. The Norwegian contest with the Kings of Scots over the
2 CONC  Hebrides and the Isle of Man in the middle 13th century underlies the sagas.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Heimskringla, Rognvald is Earl of Møre. He accompanies Harald Fairhair on his great expeditions to the west, to Ireland and to Scotland. Here, Rognvald's son Ivarr is killed. In compensation King Harald grants Rognvald Orkn
2 CONC ey and Shetland. Rognvald himself returns to Norway, giving the northern isles to his brother Sigurd Eysteinsson.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Heimskringla recounts other tales of Rognvald. It tells how he causes Harald Finehair to be given his byname Fairhair by cutting and dressing his hair, which had been uncut for ten years on account of Harald's vow never to cut
2 CONC  it until he was ruler of all Norway,[3] and it makes him the father of Ganger-Hrólf, identified by saga writers with the Rollo (Hrólfr), ancestor of the Dukes of Normandy, who was said to have been established as Count of Rouen b
2 CONC y King Charles the Simple in 931.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Rognvald is killed by Harald's son Halfdan Hålegg. Rognvald's death is avenged by his son, Earl Turf-Einar, from whom later Orkney earls claimed descent, who kills Halfdan on North Ronaldsay.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norvegiae's account of Rognvald and the foundation of the Orkney earldom is the next oldest, probably dating from the 12th century. This account contains much curious detail on Orkney, including the earliest account o
2 CONC f the Picts as small people who hid in the daytime, but it has little to say about Rognvald.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the days of Harald Fairhair, king of Norway, certain pirates, of the family of the most vigorous prince Ronald [Rognvald], set out with a great fleet, and crossed the Solundic sea..., and subdued the islands to themselves. And 
2 CONC being there provided with safe winter seats, they went in summer-time working tyranny upon the English, and the Scots, and sometimes also upon the Irish, so that they took under their rule, from England, Northumbria; from Scotland
2 CONC , Caithness; from Ireland, Dublin, and the other sea-side towns.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT This account does not associate Rognvald with the earldom, but instead attributes it to his anonymous kinfolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT The oldest account of the Rognvald and the earldom of Orkney is that found in the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland. The annals survive only in incomplete copies made by Dubhaltach Mac Fhirbhisigh in the 17th century, but the original
2 CONC  annals are believed to date from the lifetime of Donnchad mac Gilla Pátraic (died 1039). The annals are known to have had an influence on later writings in Iceland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The annals make Rognvald the son of "Halfdan, King of Lochlann". This is generally understood to mean Halfdan the Black, which would make the Rognvald of the annals the brother of Harald Finehair. However, the sagas claim that Rog
2 CONC nvald's grandfather was named Halfdan.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT These events are placed after an account of the devastation of Fortriu, dated to around 866,[8] and the fall of York, reliably dated to late 867. However, such an early date makes it difficult to reconcile the saga claims that Har
2 CONC ald Fairhair was involved in Rognvald's conquest of the northern isles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald Finehair's victory in the Battle of Hafrsfjord, which gave him dominion over parts of Norway, is traditionally dated to 872, but was probably later, perhaps as late as 900.[9] What little is known of Scottish events in the 
2 CONC period from the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba would correspond equally well with Harald's attacks on Scotland in the reign of Domnall mac Causantín (ruled 889–900).[10] However, this would not correspond with the sequence in the 
2 CONC earliest account of the origins of the Orkney earldom, which places this a generation earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rognvald_Eysteinsson
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ragnvald I 'the Wise' Eysteinsson, Earl of More (1) M, #104777 Last Edited=28 Oct 2002
2 CONT      Ragnvald I 'the Wise' Eysteinsson, Earl of More is the son of Eystein 'the Noisy' Glumra, Jarl of the Uplanders and Ascrida Ragnvaldsdottir. (1) He died circa 890, burnt to death in his house.
2 CONT      Ragnvald I 'the Wise' Eysteinsson, Earl of More gained the title of Earl of More. Ragnvald I 'the Wise' Eysteinsson, Earl of More also went by the nick-name of 'The Mighty'. Children of Ragnvald I 'the Wise' Eysteinsson, Earl
2 CONC  of More and Groa (?) -1. Hallad Ragnvaldsson, Earl of Orkney (1) -2. Turf-Einar Ragnvaldsson, Earl of Orkney (1) -3. Hrollaug Ragnvaldsson (1) Children of Ragnvald I 'the Wise' Eysteinsson, Earl of More and Ragnhild Hrolfsdottir 
2 CONC -1. Ivar Ragnvaldsson (1) -2. Thori 'the Silent' Ragnvaldsson, Jarl of More+ (1) -3. Rollo Ragnvaldsson, 1st Duc de Normandie+ b. c 846, d. c 931 (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás: http://www.thepeerage.com/p10478.htm#i104777
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
