2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/8c/78/82/d1/53444862e3c5a04c/function_of_tghe_ynglinga_saga_original.pdf?hash=fa23b83411b2e1a9049d84dd3b8bfcb1d64a1a07f6b40e4e1db99da5cb5f66ae.1797753599
2 FORM pdf
2 TITL function of the ynglinga saga.pdf
1 NOTE First King of Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of the historical Harald. The only contemporary sources mentioning him are the two skaldic poems Haraldskvæði and Glymdrápa, by Þorbjörn Hornklofi. The first poem describes life at Harald's court, mentions that he 
2 CONC took a Danish wife, and that he won a victory at Hafrsfjord. The second relates a series of battles Harald has won. He is not mentioned in any contemporary foreign sources. His life was described in several of the Kings' sagas, bu
2 CONC t the first of these were not written until the end of the 12th century, over 250 years after his death. Their accounts of Harald and his life differ on several points, and much of the content is clearly mythological. He is credit
2 CONC ed with having unified Norway into one kingdom. Modern historians assume that his rule was limited to the coastal areas of southern Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES
2 CONT Norges kongesagaer av Snorre Sturluson (1179 - 1241)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Harald Hårfagre
2 CONT Konge ca år 870 til 933.
2 CONT
2 CONT Etterkommere av Harald Hårfagre er tatt ut av hefte til Ola Aurenes om "Selvåg-ætta II" fra juli 1938.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. HARALD'S STRIFE WITH HAKE AND HIS FATHER GANDALF.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald (1) was but ten years old when he succeeded his father (Halfdan the Black). He became a stout, strong, and comely man,
2 CONT and withal prudent and manly. His mother's brother, Guthorm, was leader of the hird, at the head of the government, and commander
2 CONT (`hertogi') of the army. After Halfdan the Black's death, many chiefs coveted the dominions he had left. Among these King
2 CONT Gandalf was the first; then Hogne and Frode, sons of Eystein, king of Hedemark; and also Hogne Karuson came from Ringerike.
2 CONT Hake, the son of Gandalf, began with an expedition of 300 men against Vestfold, marched by the main road through some valleys,
2 CONT and expected to come suddenly upon King Harald; while his father Gandalf sat at home with his army, and prepared to cross over the
2 CONT fiord into Vestfold. When Duke Guthorm heard of this he gathered an army, and marched up the country with King Harald against
2 CONT Hake. They met in a valley, in which they fought a great battle, and King Harald was victorious; and there fell King Hake and most
2 CONT of his people. The place has since been called Hakadale. Then King Harald and Duke Guthorm turned back, but they found King
2 CONT Gandalf had come to Vestfold. The two armies marched against each other, and met, and had a great battle; and it ended in King
2 CONT Gandalf flying, after leaving most of his men dead on the spot, and in that state he came back to his kingdom. Now when the sons
2 CONT of King Eystein in Hedemark heard the news, they expected the war would come upon them, and they sent a message to Hogne Karuson
2 CONT and to Herse Gudbrand, and appointed a meeting with them at Ringsaker in Hedemark.
2 CONT
2 CONT ENDNOTES:
2 CONT (1) The first twenty chapters of this saga refer to Harald's youth and his conquest of Norway. This portion of the saga
2 CONT is of great importance to the Icelanders, as the settlement of their Isle was a result of Harald's wars. The second
2 CONT part of the saga (chaps. 21-46) treats of the disputes between Harald's sons, of the jarls of Orkney, and of the
2 CONT jarls of More. With this saga we enter the domain of history. -- Ed.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. KING HARALD OVERCOMES FIVE KINGS.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the battle King Harald and Guthorm turned back, and went with all the men they could gather through the forests towards
2 CONT the Uplands. They found out where the Upland kings had appointed their meeting-place, and came there about the time of midnight,
2 CONT without the watchmen observing them until their army was before the door of the house in which Hogne Karuson was, as well as that
2 CONT in which Gudbrand slept. They set fire to both houses; but King Eystein's two sons slipped out with their men, and fought for a
2 CONT while, until both Hogne and Frode fell. After the fall of these four chiefs, King Harald, by his relation Guthorm's success and
2 CONT powers, subdued Hedemark, Ringerike, Gudbrandsdal, Hadeland, Thoten, Raumarike, and the whole northern part of Vingulmark.
2 CONT King Harald and Guthorm had thereafter war with King Gandalf, and fought several battles with him; and in the last of them King
2 CONT Gandalf was slain, and King Harald took the whole of his kingdom as far south as the river Raum.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. OF GYDA, DAUGHTER OF EIRIE.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald sent his men to a girl called Gyda, daughter of King Eirik of Hordaland, who was brought up as foster-child in the
2 CONT house of a great bonde in Valdres. The king wanted her for his concubine; for she was a remarkably handsome girl, but of high
2 CONT spirit withal. Now when the messengers came there, and delivered their errand to the girl, she answered, that she would not throw
2 CONT herself away even to take a king for her husband, who had no greater kingdom to rule over than a few districts. "And
2 CONT methinks," said she, "it is wonderful that no king here in Norway will make the whole country subject to him, in the same way as
2 CONT Gorm the Old did in Denmark, or Eirik at Upsala." The messengers thought her answer was dreadfully haughty, and asked what she
2 CONT thought would come of such an answer; for Harald was so mighty a man, that his invitation was good enough for her. But although
2 CONT she had replied to their errand differently from what they wished, they saw no chance, on this occasion, of taking her with
2 CONT them against her will; so they prepared to return. When they were ready, and the people followed them out, Gyda said to the
2 CONT messengers, "Now tell to King Harald these my words. I will only agree to be his 1awful wife upon the condition that he shall
2 CONT first, for my sake, subject to himself the whole of Norway, so that he may rule over that kingdom as freely and fully as King
2 CONT Eirik over the Swedish dominions, or King Gorm over Denmark; for only then, methinks, can he be called the king of a people."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. KING HARALD'S VOW.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now came the messengers back to King Harald, bringing him the words of the girl, and saying she was so bold and foolish that
2 CONT she well deserved that the king should send a greater troop of people for her, and inflict on her some disgrace. Then answered
2 CONT the king, "This girl has not spoken or done so much amiss that she should be punished, but rather she should be thanked for her
2 CONT words. She has reminded me," said he, "of something which it appears to me wonderful I did not think of before. And now,"
2 CONT added he, "I make the solemn vow, and take God to witness, who made me and rules over all things, that never shall I clip or
2 CONT comb my hair until I have subdued the whole of Norway, with scat (1), and duties, and domains; or if not, have died in the
2 CONT attempt." Guthorm thanked the king warmly for his vow; adding, that it was royal work to fulfil royal words.
2 CONT
2 CONT ENDNOTES:
2 CONT (1) Scat was a land-tax, paid to the king in money, malt, meal, or flesh-meat, from all lands, and was adjudged by the Thing
2 CONT to each king upon his accession, and being proposed and accepted as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. THE BATTLE IN ORKADAL.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this the two relations gather together a great force, and prepare for an expedition to the Uplands, and northwards up the
2 CONT valley (Gudbrandsdal), and north over Dovrefjeld; and when the king came down to the inhabited land he ordered all the men to be
2 CONT killed, and everything wide around to be delivered to the flames. And when the people came to know this, they fled every one where
2 CONT he could; some down the country to Orkadal, some to Gaulardal, some to the forests. But some begged for peace, and obtained it,
2 CONT on condition of joining the king and becoming his men. He met no opposition until he came to Orkadal. There a crowd of people had
2 CONT assembled, and he had his first battle with a king called Gryting. Harald won the victory, and King Gryting was made
2 CONT prisoner, and most of his people killed. He took service himself under the king, and swore fidelity to him. Thereafer all the
2 CONT people in Orkadal district went under King Harald, and became his men.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. KING HARALD S LAWS FOR LAND PROPERTY.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald made this law over all the lands he conquered, that all the udal property should belong to him; and that the bondes,
2 CONT both great and small, should pay him land dues for their possessions. Over every district he set an earl to judge
2 CONT according to the law of the land and to justice, and also to collect the land dues and the fines; and for this each earl
2 CONT received a third part of the dues, and services, and fines, for the support of his table and other expenses. Each earl had under
2 CONT him four or more herses, each of whom had an estate of twenty marks yearly income bestowed on him and was bound to support
2 CONT twenty men-at-arms, and the earl sixty men, at their own expenses. The king had increased the land dues and burdens so
2 CONT much, that each of his earls had greater power and income than the kings had before; and when that became known at Throndhjem,
2 CONT many great men joined the king and took his service.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. BATTLE IN GAULARDAL.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is told that Earl Hakon Grjotgardson came to King Harald from Yrjar, and brought a great crowd of men to his service. Then
2 CONT King Harald went into Gaulardal, and had a great battle, in which he slew two kings, and conquered their dominions; and these were
2 CONT Gaulardal district and Strind district. He gave Earl Hakon Strind district to rule over as earl. King Harald then proceeded
2 CONT to Stjoradal, and had a third battle, in which he gained the victory, and took that district also. There upon the Throndhjem
2 CONT people assembled, and four kings met together with their troops. The one ruled over Veradal, the second over Skaun, third over the
2 CONT Sparbyggja district, and the fourth over Eyin Idre (Inderoen); and this latter had also Eyna district. These four kings marched
2 CONT with their men against King Harald, but he won the battle; and some of these kings fell, and some fled. In all, King Harald
2 CONT fought at the least eight battles, and slew eight kings, in the Throndhjem district, and laid the whole of it under him.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. HARALD SEIZES NAUMUDAL DISTRICT.
2 CONT
2 CONT North in Naumudal were two brothers, kings, -- Herlaug and Hrollaug; and they had been for three summers raising a mound or
2 CONT tomb of stone and lime and of wood. Just as the work was finished, the brothers got the news that King Harald was coming
2 CONT upon them with his army. Then King Herlaug had a great quantity of meat and drink brought into the mound, and went into it
2 CONT himself, with eleven companions, and ordered the mound to be covered up. King Hrollaug, on the contrary, went upon the summit
2 CONT of the mound, on which the kings were wont to sit, and made a throne to be erected, upon which he seated himself. Then he
2 CONT ordered feather-beds to be laid upon the bench below, on which the earls were wont to be seated, and threw himself down from his
2 CONT high seat or throne into the earl's seat, giving himself the title of earl. Now Hrollaug went to meet King Harald, gave up to
2 CONT him his whole kingdom, offered to enter into his service, and told him his whole proceeding. Then took King Harald a sword,
2 CONT fastened it to Hrollaug's belt, bound a shield to his neck, and made him thereupon an earl, and led him to his earl's seat; and
2 CONT therewith gave him the district Naumudal, and set him as earl over it ((A.D. 866)). (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT ENDNOTES:
2 CONT (1) Before writing was in general use, this symbolical way of performing all important legal acts appears to have entered
2 CONT into the jurisprudence of all savage nations; and according to Gibbon, chap. 44, "the jurisprudence of the first Romans
2 CONT exhibited the scenes of a pantomime; the words were adapted to the gestures, and the slightest error or neglect in the
2 CONT forms of proceeding was sufficient to annul the substance of the fairest claims." -- Ed.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. KING HARALD'S HOME AFFAIRS.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald then returned to Throndhjem, where he dwelt during the winter, and always afterwards called it his home. He fixed
2 CONT here his head residence, which is called Lade. This winter he took to wife Asa, a daughter of Earl Hakon Grjotgardson, who then
2 CONT stood in great favour and honour with the king. In spring the king fitted out his ships. In winter he had caused a great
2 CONT frigate (a dragon) to be built, and had it fitted-out in the most splendid way, and brought his house-troops and his berserks on
2 CONT board. The forecastle men were picked men, for they had the king's banner. From the stem to the mid-hold was called rausn,
2 CONT or the fore-defence; and there were the berserks. Such men only were received into King Harald's house-troop as were remarkable
2 CONT for strength, courage, and all kinds of dexterity; and they alone got place in his ship, for he had a good choice of house-troops
2 CONT from the best men of every district. King Harald had a great army, many large ships, and many men of might followed him.
2 CONT Hornklofe, in his poem called "Glymdrapa", tells of this; and also that King Harald had a battle with the people of Orkadal, at
2 CONT Opdal forest, before he went upon this expedition.
2 CONT
2 CONT "O'er the broad heath the bowstrings twang,
2 CONT While high in air the arrows sang.
2 CONT The iron shower drives to flight
2 CONT The foeman from the bloody fight.
2 CONT The warder of great Odin's shrine,
2 CONT The fair-haired son of Odin's line,
2 CONT Raises the voice which gives the cheer,
2 CONT First in the track of wolf or bear.
2 CONT His master voice drives them along
2 CONT To Hel -- a destined, trembling throng;
2 CONT And Nokve's ship, with glancing sides,
2 CONT Must fly to the wild ocean's tides. --
2 CONT Must fly before the king who leads
2 CONT Norse axe-men on their ocean steeds."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. BATTLE AT SOLSKEL
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald moved out with his army from Throndhjem, and went southwards to More. Hunthiof was the name of the king who ruled
2 CONT over the district of More. Solve Klofe was the name of his son, and both were great warriors. King Nokve, who ruled over
2 CONT Raumsdal, was the brother of Solve's mother. Those chiefs gathered a great force when they heard of King Harald, and came
2 CONT against him. They met at Solskel, and there was a great battle, which was gained by King Harald (A.D. 867). Hornklofe tells of this battle: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thus did the hero known to fame,
2 CONT The leader of the shields, whose name
2 CONT Strikes every heart with dire dismay,
2 CONT Launch forth his war-ships to the fray.
2 CONT Two kings he fought; but little strife
2 CONT Was needed to cut short their life.
2 CONT A clang of arms by the sea-shore, --
2 CONT And the shields' sound was heard no more."
2 CONT
2 CONT The two kings were slain, but Solve escaped by flight; and King Harald laid both districts under his power. He stayed here long
2 CONT in summer to establish law and order for the country people, and set men to rule them, and keep them faithful to him; and in
2 CONT autumn he prepared to return northwards to Throndhjem. Ragnvald Earl of More, a son of Eystein Glumra, had the summer before
2 CONT become one of Harald's men; and the king set him as chief over these two districts, North More and Raumsdal; strengthened him
2 CONT both with men of might and bondes, and gave him the help of ships to defend the coast against enemies. He was called
2 CONT Ragnvald the Mighty, or the Wise; and people say both names suited him well. King Harald came back to Throndhjem about winter.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. FALL OF KINGS ARNVID AND AUDBJORN.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following spring (A.D. 868) King Harald raised a great force in Throndhjem, and gave out that he would proceed to South More.
2 CONT Solve Klofe had passed the winter in his ships of war, plundering in North More, and had killed many of King Harald's men;
2 CONT pillaging some places, burning others, and making great ravage; but sometimes he had been, during the winter, with his friend
2 CONT King Arnvid in South More. Now when he heard that King Harald was come with ships and a great army, he gathered people, and was
2 CONT strong in men-at-arms; for many thought they had to take vengeance of King Harald. Solve Klofe went southwards to
2 CONT Firdafylke (the Fjord district), which King Audbjorn ruled over, to ask him to help, and join his force to King Arnvid's and his
2 CONT own. "For," said he, "it is now clear that we all have but one course to take; and that is to rise, all as one man, against King
2 CONT Harald, for we have strength enough, and fate must decide the victory; for as to the other condition of becoming his servants,
2 CONT that is no condition for us, who are not less noble than Harald. My father thought it better to fall in battle for his kingdom,
2 CONT than to go willingly into King Harald's service, or not to abide the chance of weapons like the Naumudal kings." King Solve's
2 CONT speech was such that King Audbjorn promised his help, and gathered a great force together and went with it to King Arnvid,
2 CONT and they had a great army. Now, they got news that King Harald was come from the north, and they met within Solskel. And it was
2 CONT the custom to lash the ships together, stem to stem; so it was done now. King Harald laid his ship against King Arnvid's, and
2 CONT there was the sharpest fight, and many men fell on both sides. At last King Harald was raging with anger, and went forward to
2 CONT the fore-deck, and slew so dreadfully that all the forecastle men of Arnvid's ship were driven aft of the mast, and some fell.
2 CONT Thereupon Harald boarded the ship, and King Arnvid's men tried to save themselves by flight, and he himself was slain in his ship.
2 CONT King Audbjorn also fell; but Solve fled. So says Hornklofe: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Against the hero's shield in vain
2 CONT The arrow-storm fierce pours its rain.
2 CONT The king stands on the blood-stained deck,
2 CONT Trampling on many a stout foe's neck;
2 CONT And high above the dinning stound
2 CONT Of helm and axe, and ringing sound
2 CONT Of blade and shield, and raven's cry,
2 CONT Is heard his shout of `Victory!'"
2 CONT
2 CONT Of King Harald's men, fell his earls Asgaut and Asbjorn, together with his brothers-in-law, Grjotgard and Herlaug, the sons of Earl
2 CONT Hakon of Lade. Solve became afterwards a great sea-king, and often did great damage in King Harald's dominions.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 12. KING VEMUND BURNT TO DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this battle (A.D. 868) King Harald subdued South More; but Vemund, King Audbjorn's brother, still had Firdafylke. It was
2 CONT now late in harvest, and King Harald's men gave him the counsel not to proceed south-wards round Stad. Then King Harald set Earl
2 CONT Ragnvald over South and North More and also Raumsdal, and he had many people about him. King Harald returned to Throndhjem. The
2 CONT same winter (A.D. 869) Ragnvald went over Eid, and southwards to the Fjord district. There he heard news of King Vemund, and came
2 CONT by night to a place called Naustdal, where King Vemund was living in guest-quarters. Earl Ragnvald surrounded the house in which
2 CONT they were quartered, and burnt the king in it, together with ninety men. The came Berdlukare to Earl Ragnvald with a complete
2 CONT armed long-ship, and they both returned to More. The earl took all the ships Vemund had, and all the goods he could get hold of.
2 CONT Berdlukare proceeded north to Throndhjem to King Harald, and became his man; and dreadful berserk he was.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 13. DEATH OF EARLS HAKON, AND ATLE MJOVE.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following spring (A.D. 869) King Harald went southwards with his fleet along the coast, and subdued Firdafylke. Then he
2 CONT sailed eastward along the land until he came to Vik; but he left Earl Hakon Grjotgardson behind, and set him over the Fjord
2 CONT district. Earl Hakon sent word to Earl Atle Mjove that he should leave Sogn district, and be earl over Gaular district, as he had
2 CONT been before, alleging that King Harald had given Sogn district to him. Earl Atle sent word that he would keep both Sogn district
2 CONT and Gaular district, until he met King Harald. The two earls quarreled about this so long, that both gathered troops. They
2 CONT met at Fialar, in Stavanger fiord, and had a great battle, in which Earl Hakon fell, and Earl Atle got a mortal wound, and his
2 CONT men carried him to the island of Atley, where he died. So says Eyvind Skaldaspiller: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "He who stood a rooted oak,
2 CONT Unshaken by the swordsman's stroke,
2 CONT Amidst the whiz of arrows slain,
2 CONT Has fallen upon Fjalar's plain.
2 CONT There, by the ocean's rocky shore,
2 CONT The waves are stained with the red gore
2 CONT Of stout Earl Hakon Grjotgard's son,
2 CONT And of brave warriors many a one."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 14. HARALD AND THE SWEDISH KING EIRIK.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald came with his fleet eastward to Viken and landed at Tunsberg, which was then a trading town. He had then been four
2 CONT years in Throndhjem, and in all that time had not been in Viken. Here he heard the news that Eirik Eymundson, king of Sweden, had
2 CONT laid under him Vermaland, and was taking scat or land-tax from all the forest settlers; and also that he called the whole
2 CONT country north to Svinasund, and west along the sea, West Gautland; and which altogether he reckoned to his kingdom, and
2 CONT took land-tax from it. Over this country he had set an earl, by name Hrane Gauzke, who had the earldom between Svinasund and the
2 CONT Gaut river, and was a mighty earl. And it was told to King Harald that the Swedish king said he would not rest until he had
2 CONT as great a kingdom in Viken as Sigurd Hring, or his son Ragnar Lodbrok, had possessed; and that was Raumarike and Vestfold, all
2 CONT the way to the isle Grenmar, and also Vingulmark, and all that lay south of it. In all these districts many chiefs, and many
2 CONT other people, had given obedience to the Swedish king. King Harald was very angry at this, and summoned the bondes to a Thing
2 CONT at Fold, where he laid an accusation against them for treason towards him. Some bondes defended themselves from the
2 CONT accusation, some paid fines, some were punished. He went thus through the whole district during the summer, and in harvest he
2 CONT did the same in Raumarike, and laid the two districts under his power. Towards winter he heard that Eirik king of Sweden was,
2 CONT with his court, going about in Vermaland in guest-quarters.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 15. HARALD AT A FEAST OF THE PEASANT AKE.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald takes his way across the Eid forest eastward, and comes out in Vermaland, where he also orders feasts to be
2 CONT prepared for himself. There was a man by name Ake, who was the greatest of the bondes of Vermaland, very rich, and at that time
2 CONT very aged. He sent men to King Harald, and invited him to a feast, and the king promised to come on the day appointed. Ake
2 CONT invited also King Eirik to a feast, and appointed the same day. Ake had a great feasting hall, but it was old; and he made a new
2 CONT hall, not less than the old one, and had it ornamented in the most splendid way. The new hall he had hung with new hangings,
2 CONT but the old had only its old ornaments. Now when the kings came to the feast, King Eirik with his court was taken into the old
2 CONT hall; but Harald with his followers into the new. The same difference was in all the table furniture, and King Eirik and his
2 CONT men had the old-fashioned vessels and horns, but all gilded and splendid; while King Harald and his men had entirely new vessels
2 CONT and horns adorned with gold, all with carved figures, and shining like glass; and both companies had the best of liquor. Ake the
2 CONT bonde had formerly been King Halfdan the Black s man. Now when daylight came, and the feast was quite ended, and the kings made
2 CONT themselves ready for their journey, and the horses were saddled, came Ake before King Harald, leading in his hand his son Ubbe, a
2 CONT boy of twelve years of age, and said, "If the goodwill I have shown to thee, sire, in my feast, be worth thy friendship, show
2 CONT it hereafter to my son. I give him to thee now for thy service." The king thanked him with many agreeable words for his friendly
2 CONT entertainment, and promised him his full friendship in return. Then Ake brought out great presents, which he gave to the king,
2 CONT and they gave each other thereafter the parting kiss. Ake went next to the Swedish king, who was dressed and ready for the road,
2 CONT but not in the best humour. Ake gave to him also good and valuable gifts; but the king answered only with few words, and
2 CONT mounted his horse. Ake followed the king on the road and talked with him. The road led through a wood which was near to the
2 CONT house; and when Ake came to the wood, the king said to him, "How was it that thou madest such a difference between me and King
2 CONT Harald as to give him the best of everything, although thou knowest thou art my man?" "I think" answered Ake, "that there
2 CONT failed in it nothing, king, either to you or to your attendants, in friendly entertainment at this feast. But that all the
2 CONT utensils for your drinking were old, was because you are now old; but King Harald is in the bloom of youth, and therefore I gave
2 CONT him the new things. And as to my being thy man, thou art just as much my man." On this the king out with his sword, and gave Ake
2 CONT his deathwound. King Harald was ready now also to mount his horse, and desired that Ake should be called. The people went to
2 CONT seek him; and some ran up the road that King Eirik had taken, and found Ake there dead. They came back, and told the news to King
2 CONT Harald, and he bids his men to be up, and avenge Ake the bonde. And away rode he and his men the way King Eirik had taken, until
2 CONT they came in sight of each other. Each for himself rode as hard as he could, until Eirik came into the wood which divides
2 CONT Gautland and Vermaland. There King Harald wheels about, and returns to Vermaland, and lays the country under him, and kills
2 CONT King Eirik's men wheresoever he can find them. In winter King Harald returned to Raumarike, and dwelt there a while.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 16. HARALD'S JOURNEY TO TUNSBERG.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald went out in winter to his ships at Tunsberg, rigged them, and sailed away eastward over the fiord, and subjected all
2 CONT Vingulmark to his dominion. All winter he was out with his ships, and marauded in Ranrike; so says Thorbjorn Hornklofe: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Norseman's king is on the sea,
2 CONT Tho' bitter wintry cold it be. --
2 CONT On the wild waves his Yule keeps he.
2 CONT When our brisk king can get his way,
2 CONT He'll no more by the fireside stay
2 CONT Than the young sun; he makes us play
2 CONT The game of the bright sun-god Frey.
2 CONT But the soft Swede loves well the fire
2 CONT The well-stuffed couch, the doway glove,
2 CONT And from the hearth-seat will not move."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gautlanders gathered people together all over the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. THE BATTLE IN GAUTLAND.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spring, when the ice was breaking up, the Gautlanders drove stakes into the Gaut river to hinder King Harald with his ships
2 CONT from coming to the land. But King Harald laid his ships alongside the stakes, and plundered the country, and burnt all around; so says Horn klofe: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "The king who finds a dainty feast,
2 CONT For battle-bird and prowling beast,
2 CONT Has won in war the southern land
2 CONT That lies along the ocean's strand.
2 CONT The leader of the helmets, he
2 CONT Who leads his ships o'er the dark sea,
2 CONT Harald, whose high-rigged masts appear
2 CONT Like antlered fronts of the wild deer,
2 CONT Has laid his ships close alongside
2 CONT Of the foe's piles with daring pride."
2 CONT
2 CONT Afterwards the Gautlanders came down to the strand with a great army, and gave battle to King Harald, and great was the fall of men.
2 CONT But it was King Harald who gained the day. Thus says Hornklofe: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Whistles the battle-axe in its swing
2 CONT O'er head the whizzing javelins sing,
2 CONT Helmet and shield and hauberk ring;
2 CONT The air-song of the lance is loud,
2 CONT The arrows pipe in darkening cloud;
2 CONT Through helm and mail the foemen feel
2 CONT The blue edge of our king's good steel
2 CONT Who can withstand our gallant king?
2 CONT The Gautland men their flight must wing."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 18. HRANE GAUZKE'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald went far and wide through Gautland, and many were the battles he fought there on both sides of the river, and in
2 CONT general he was victorious. In one of these battles fell Hrane Gauzke; and then the king took his whole land north of the river
2 CONT and west of the Veneren, and also Vermaland. And after he turned back there-from, he set Duke Guthorm as chief to defend the
2 CONT country, and left a great force with him. King Harald himself went first to the Uplands, where he remained a while, and then
2 CONT proceeded northwards over the Dovrefjeld to Trondhjem, where he dwelt for a long time. Harald began to have children. By Asa he
2 CONT had four sons. The eldest was Guthorm. Halfdan the Black and Halfdan the White were twins. Sigfrod was the fourth. They were
2 CONT all brought up in Trondhjem with all honour.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 19. BATTLE IN HAFRSFJORD.
2 CONT
2 CONT News came in from the south land that the people of Hordaland and Rogaland, Agder and Telemark, were gathering, and bringing
2 CONT together ships and weapons, and a great body of men. The leaders of this were Eirik king of Hordaland; Sulke king of Rogaland, and
2 CONT his brother Earl Sote: Kjotve the Rich, king of Agder, and his son Thor Haklang; and from Telemark two brothers, Hroald Hryg
2 CONT and Had the Hard. Now when Harald got certain news of this, he assembled his forces, set his ships on the water, made himself
2 CONT ready with his men, and set out southwards along the coast, gathering many people from every district. King Eirik heard of
2 CONT this when he same south of Stad; and having assembled all the men he could expect, he proceeded southwards to meet the force which
2 CONT he knew was coming to his help from the east. The whole met together north of Jadar, and went into Hafersfjord, where King
2 CONT Harald was waiting with his forces. A great battle began, which was both hard and long; but at last King Harald gained the day.
2 CONT There King Eirik fell, and King Sulke, with his brother Earl Sote. Thor Haklang, who was a great berserk, had laid his ship
2 CONT against King Harald's, and there was above all measure a desperate attack, until Thor Haklang fell, and his whole ship was
2 CONT cleared of men. Then King Kjotve fled to a little isle outside, on which there was a good place of strength. Thereafter all his
2 CONT men fled, some to their ships, some up to the land; and the latter ran southwards over the country of Jadar. So says Hornklove, viz.: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Has the news reached you? -- have you heard
2 CONT Of the great fight at Hafersfjord,
2 CONT Between our noble king brave Harald
2 CONT And King Kjotve rich in gold?
2 CONT The foeman came from out the East,
2 CONT Keen for the fray as for a feast.
2 CONT A gallant sight it was to see
2 CONT Their fleet sweep o'er the dark-blue sea:
2 CONT Each war-ship, with its threatening throat
2 CONT Of dragon fierce or ravenous brute (1)
2 CONT Grim gaping from the prow; its wales
2 CONT Glittering with burnished shields, (2) like scales
2 CONT Its crew of udal men of war,
2 CONT Whose snow-white targets shone from far
2 CONT And many a mailed spearman stout
2 CONT From the West countries round about,
2 CONT English and Scotch, a foreign host,
2 CONT And swordamen from the far French coast.
2 CONT
